# MIR (vendored) — in-process micro-JIT for the codegen RHS

This is a pruned vendor copy of [MIR](https://github.com/vnmakarov/mir) by
Vladimir Makarov, pinned at commit `439a90a2` (MIT-licensed; see `LICENSE`),
with three local carries, two for issue #1215 and one for issue #1216 (see
`bngsim/scripts/MIR_VENDORING.md`).
MIR's own `README.md` is preserved alongside this file.

## Why it's here (GH #78)

bngsim's codegen backend emits good native-quality C for the ODE RHS
(`bngsim/python/bngsim/_codegen.py`), but pays a heavy *compile latency*:
`cc -O3` as a subprocess plus `dlopen` costs ~80 ms–seconds. That latency is
why the codegen threshold sits at N≥256 species and leaves the intermediate
band on the slower ExprTk-bytecode path.

MIR ships **c2mir**, a C11 frontend, so the *same C source `_codegen.py`
already emits* can be JIT-compiled in-process in ~1–2 ms — no subprocess, no
`.so`, no `dlopen`. It is a single ~1 MB C library (no LLVM, no Rust toolchain),
targeting x86-64 and aarch64 (Apple Silicon).

## What is built

Three translation units, gated behind the `BNGSIM_ENABLE_MIR` CMake option
(default OFF):

- `mir.c`        — MIR core (loader, linker, interpreter; `#include`s the
                   host target backend, e.g. `mir-x86_64.c`/`mir-aarch64.c`)
- `mir-gen.c`    — the optimizing JIT code generator
- `c2mir/c2mir.c`— the C11 → MIR frontend

Include dirs: this directory and `c2mir/`.

## The header-free-source rule

c2mir's bundled preprocessor cannot parse the platform SDK `<math.h>` /
`<stdlib.h>` — they use compiler-specific extensions (on macOS c2mir reports
*"Unsupported compiler detected"* and chokes on the SDK `math.h`).

Because bngsim *generates* the C it JITs, the JIT path sidesteps this entirely:
it strips the `#include <...>` lines from the generated source and prepends
`extern` declarations for the libc/libm symbols the RHS can call (`pow`, `exp`,
`sqrt`, `log`, `fabs`, `fmax`, `fmin`, `memset`, …). MIR resolves those externals
at link time through the import resolver, which calls `dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT, name)`.
See `bngsim/include/bngsim/mir_jit.hpp`.

## Refreshing

Use `bngsim/scripts/vendor_mir.py` — it prunes the tree from a MIR checkout at
the pinned commit in `VENDOR.json` and re-anchors the per-file checksums.
`--summary` previews, `--check` is the offline tripwire that the checked-in tree
still matches `VENDOR.json`. See `bngsim/scripts/MIR_VENDORING.md` for the full
refresh + MIR-on build/validation flow.

## The code reservation (issue #1215)

Each MIR context reserves one stretch of address space for its generated code,
`MIR_CODE_RESERVE_SIZE`, so that the code's direct branches reach each other
(±128 MB on aarch64); code that outgrows it is mapped on its own. Upstream
reserves 128 MB on 64-bit hosts other than Windows. That is address space, not
memory, but a cap on the address space (`ulimit -v`, `RLIMIT_AS`, as some
clusters set) counts it: with 128 MB, about 60 live MIR-JIT'd models filled
8 GB. bngsim's CMake sets it to `BNGSIM_MIR_CODE_RESERVE_MB`, 32 MB by default,
which holds the largest committed model's code in one piece (fceri_gamma's
27.9 MB, measured on macOS arm64). bngsim generates code eagerly, and its calls
and thunk redirects check their reach, so code mapped on its own runs the same:
the size is address space only. Under a tight cap, configure with
`-DBNGSIM_MIR_CODE_RESERVE_MB=8` (fceri_fyn's 7.4 MB fits), or `0` to reserve
nothing. The value is a CMake cache entry, so a `pip`/`uv` install keeps the
last one passed in its build directory. `scripts/rebuild_editable.py` passes
32 on every rebuild instead (GH #459); set `BNGSIM_MIR_CODE_RESERVE_MB` in its
environment to ask for another. Windows reserves nothing either way.

## Running out (issue #1215)

At a cap on the address space, the model that does not fit fails to compile.
MIR's own answer to that was to end the process: its default error function
and its growable arrays call `exit(1)`, and a failed code mapping comes back as
a NULL code address that MIR writes through. `MirJit` therefore hands each
context an allocator, a code allocator wrapping MIR's default one, and an error
function, all of which return to `MirJit::compile` by `longjmp` (MIR.md asks only
that an error function never return), and `MirJit` raises a `RuntimeError`. MIR cannot finish a context it
failed in, so `MirJit` abandons it: its heap memory stays allocated, but its code
mappings are unmapped. Two local carries make this reachable: `mir.c` exposes the
default code allocator to wrap, and `c2mir.c` reports, through MIR's error
function, a failure of the three records its preprocessor allocates with plain
`malloc`.

For a large model the reservation is not most of what a live MIR context holds
against such a cap: its compiled MIR is. `MirJit` finishes c2mir as soon as it
has made the module, which took a live fceri_fyn context from 852 MB of heap to
388 MB (macOS arm64). A small one holds a few MB (simple_decay 1, egfr_simple_seed
6), so the reservation is still most of it.
